    n<// PIC Data Memory addressing is complicated.  See the Data Book for full explanation..> u<9350> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20732> s<9351> l<1241:1> el<1241:88>
    n<//> u<9351> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20732> s<9352> l<1242:1> el<1242:3>
    n<// Basically, each BANK contains 32 locations.  The lowest 16 locations in ALL Banks> u<9352> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20732> s<9353> l<1243:1> el<1243:85>
    n<// are really all mapped to the same bank (bank #0).  The first 8 locations are the Special> u<9353> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20732> s<9354> l<1244:1> el<1244:92>
    n<// registers like the STATUS and PC registers.  The upper 16 words in each bank, really are> u<9354> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20732> s<9355> l<1245:1> el<1245:92>
    n<// unique to each bank.  The smallest PIC (16C54) only has the one bank #0.> u<9355> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20732> s<9356> l<1246:1> el<1246:76>
    n<//> u<9356> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20732> s<9357> l<1247:1> el<1247:3>
    n<// So, as a programmer, what you get is this.  No matter what bank you are in (FSR[6:5])> u<9357> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20732> s<9358> l<1248:1> el<1248:89>
    n<// you always have access to your special registers and also to registers 8-15.  You can> u<9358> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20732> s<9359> l<1249:1> el<1249:89>
    n<// change to a 1 of 4 banks by setting FSR[6:5] and get 4 different sets of registers> u<9359> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20732> s<9360> l<1250:1> el<1250:86>
    n<// 16-31.> u<9360> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20732> s<9361> l<1251:1> el<1251:10>
